    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "a86ca3ff-6251-3f2a-82e0-d3c86198eb3b",
                "word": "palmistry",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "5462a330-4a65-46b4-8aee-6a71533ebb66",
                        "entry_id": "a86ca3ff-6251-3f2a-82e0-d3c86198eb3b",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "palmistries"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "58b02046-1d61-3b4f-bad1-6689fbd83eac",
                        "entry_id": "a86ca3ff-6251-3f2a-82e0-d3c86198eb3b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Telling fortunes from the lines on the palms of the hand."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable",
                            "usually"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e56be98a-ae2d-4d1e-b6aa-fb74f1cbbf77",
                                "sense_id": "58b02046-1d61-3b4f-bad1-6689fbd83eac",
                                "text": "Chiromantie is a coniecturyng by beholdyng the lynes, or wryncles of the handes called commonly Palmistry."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5f9a3ae8-4cec-44c5-89d0-ede411578d50",
                                "sense_id": "58b02046-1d61-3b4f-bad1-6689fbd83eac",
                                "text": "And those fayre hands within whose louely palmes,\nFortune diuineth happie Augurie,\nThose straightest fingers dealing heauenly almes,\nPointed with pur’st of Natures Alcumie,\nWhere loue sits looking in loues palmistrie."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b4dd28b2-b997-46e0-80f5-d2b632923160",
                                "sense_id": "58b02046-1d61-3b4f-bad1-6689fbd83eac",
                                "text": "[…] but his [God’s] right hand of truth and bountie, does by a Catholike and vnfeigned Palmistrie, shew the blessings prouided for other men!"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "23ebe093-73c8-4546-9351-c0b0cd88589f",
                                "sense_id": "58b02046-1d61-3b4f-bad1-6689fbd83eac",
                                "text": "He must (at least) hold up his hand,\nBy twelve Free-holders to be scan’d,\nWho by their skill in Palmistry,\nWill quickly read his Destiny;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "04f8b4e4-df0d-4493-8426-bac6317d79d3",
                                "sense_id": "58b02046-1d61-3b4f-bad1-6689fbd83eac",
                                "text": "I have seen a gipsy vagabond; she has practised in hackneyed fashion the science of palmistry and told me what such people usually tell."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6b21eb28-da3a-49d0-8913-6b2997973dad",
                                "sense_id": "58b02046-1d61-3b4f-bad1-6689fbd83eac",
                                "text": "If you had cared to do so, you could have told the little chap’s fortune from those hands. They were not flat and featureless as you might have expected them to be; already they had all the lines and creases known to palmistry."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a320de74-bc0a-34f1-ae30-299727dc2564",
                        "entry_id": "a86ca3ff-6251-3f2a-82e0-d3c86198eb3b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A book on palmistry; a system of palmistry."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "usually"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f2fbb170-da04-4177-80ad-abd13d593854",
                                "sense_id": "a320de74-bc0a-34f1-ae30-299727dc2564",
                                "text": "No living palmistries can spell\nThe bird-runes on the stretched silk of her hand"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "caca3b4f-4485-4678-9c9c-f0a7119876d2",
                                "sense_id": "a320de74-bc0a-34f1-ae30-299727dc2564",
                                "text": "Both the composition and the transmission of this palmistry must be taken in the context of the medieval Christian world, in which the life on earth was still secondary to the life in the hereafter; even so, texts such as these palmistries do make the unreadable mysteries of one’s relationship to the world seem more familiar and more accessible."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8266d028-aac4-4859-958f-049116ba0544",
                                "sense_id": "a320de74-bc0a-34f1-ae30-299727dc2564",
                                "text": "1996, Richard Grossinger, New Moon, Berkeley, CA: Frog, Part 7, Chapter 2, p. 534,\nTo fulfill my graduate language requirement I began reading Michel Foucault’s work on signatures, Les Mots et Les Choses, which joined the meanings of the bestiaries, herbals, palmistries, and physiognomies of olden Europe to the totemic orders of plants and animals among the Arapaho, Xhosa, and Aranda."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "40b91dbe-7b80-31e0-93ff-8194a497c2af",
                        "entry_id": "a86ca3ff-6251-3f2a-82e0-d3c86198eb3b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A dexterous use or trick of the hand."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete",
                            "rare",
                            "uncountable",
                            "usually"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f628cb9d-c1b1-4f8b-9f78-d207971b84da",
                                "sense_id": "40b91dbe-7b80-31e0-93ff-8194a497c2af",
                                "text": "1711, Joseph Addison and Richard Steele, The Spectator, Volume 2, No. 130, 30 July, 1711, London: J. and R. Tonson, 12th edition, 1739, p. 182,\nIn the Height of his Good-humour, meeting a common Beggar upon the Road who was no Conjuror, as he went to relieve him he found his Pocket was pick’d: That being a Kind of Palmistry at which this Race of Vermin are very dextrous."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "3309fb3d-0125-439d-a74a-a0d5d8f31663",
                        "source_entry_id": "a86ca3ff-6251-3f2a-82e0-d3c86198eb3b",
                        "source_sense_description": "telling fortunes from the lines on the palms of the hand",
                        "target_lang_word": "handlijnkunde",
                        "target_lang_code": "nl",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "handlijnkunde",
                                "target_lang_code": "nl",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "78d76794-42ed-302f-a00b-f15bda8762f4",
                                        "entry_id": "3252177d-fd15-31cf-8469-6dfa19973398",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "pseudowetenschap die beweert de toekomst van iemand te kunnen lezen aan de hand van het huidlijnenpatroon in de hand"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "43347d93-4735-45f1-8ae5-cab149f4b16e",
                                                "sense_id": "78d76794-42ed-302f-a00b-f15bda8762f4",
                                                "text": "Etty had een literaire gave, en met het schrijven van een dagboek kon zij de ellende van de oorlog niet alleen van haar afschrijven maar ook spiritueel duiden en zelfs overwinnen. Dankzij het dagboek kwam zij in het reine met zichzelf en de erge bezettingsomstandigheden. Het dagboek (dat ook een verslag is van de vreemde cultus rond Spier en zijn handlijnkunde) was ook een soort zelfgeschreven cursus om tot de ultieme goedheid te komen. Geslaagd voor haar eigen cursus en met deze spirituele overwinning op zak meldde zij zich als een soort zelfbenoemde heilige in kamp Westerbork om de slachtoffer 'te helpen'. Door sommigen gezien als de ultieme nobelheid, door anderen als een extreme vorm van gekte, als de sublimatie van gekte."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
